New workflow.

There are a lot of files and patches that are only useful
for testing on LLNL machines, and a few patches that are
useful for me but don't belong upstream.  In order to
incorporate those into this repo, use this workflow.

devel           should mirror upstream/devel
my_fix          code intended for devel
devel-local     location-specific files
my_fix-local    merges devel-local and my_fix

1) devel-local should be periodically rebased on devel.

2) Coding should happen on my_fix.  Unfortunately, any
    changes need to be committed and merged into
    my_fix-local, which might get annoying.

3) Compiling and testing should happen on my_fix-local.

For example:

I have a ci.yaml file I've been working on that runs
four different builds (flux, serial, slurm, slurm-plugin)
at all four debug levels (0-3), and creates artifacts out
of all the logfiles.  This is overkill for most development
on the devel branch, so it shouldn't be enabled in devel.

I can, however, keep that ci.yaml file as the default in
devel-local and repeatedly tweak (and commit) it as needed.
That gives me a more flexible debugging environment without
adding a bunch of extraneous commits to devel.

Another example:

I have a set of LC-specific scripts for configuration,
building, installling and running Spindle.  Currently, I'm
keeping them in a separate repo, but they rarely get pushed
after modification and they're not tied to the specific
Spindle branches and versions they're used on.  Pulling
them into devel-local will help ensure changes get backed
up and will make reproducing old tests much easier.

There are, over course, other ways of doing this.  For the
environment I have in front of me, the above appears to
have the best set of tradeoffs.
